=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: sshswitch
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies on sshswitch, not on switch4 as the helpdesk ticket assumed. The
affected subsystem is the VLAN trunk termination on sshswitch's eth0 interface
(the downlink to switch4, shared segment ms1_sw4).

sshswitch acts as the Campus-2 multilayer gateway: it hosts the VLAN 5 SVI at
10.5.0.254 (bridge br_v5) and the VLAN 6 SVI at 10.6.0.254 (bridge br_v6).
For VLAN-tagged frames arriving from switch4 to reach those SVIs, sshswitch
must have VLAN subinterfaces on eth0 (eth0.5 and eth0.6) joined to the
respective bridge domains.

The original sshswitch startup script — generated by an automated Cisco-to-Linux
translation tool — omitted these subinterface commands entirely (the startup
itself documents this as a "translation bug / eth0 orphaned"). As a result,
tagged VLAN 5 and VLAN 6 frames arriving from switch4 on eth0 had no bridge
members to carry them to br_v5 or br_v6. Every 10.5.0.x and 10.6.0.x host on
switch4 was stranded at Layer 2 — ARP for the gateway went unanswered and
pings to 10.5.0.254 / 10.6.0.254 timed out completely.

Root cause: sshswitch's eth0 trunk port was missing VLAN 5 and VLAN 6
subinterface entries in its startup configuration, severing the Layer-2 path
between switch4's VLAN bridges and sshswitch's gateway SVIs.

Corrective fix (now applied in the running state): add `ip link add link eth0
name eth0.5 type vlan id 5`, attach eth0.5 to br_v5; repeat for eth0.6 and
br_v6; keep bare eth0 in br_v1 for native VLAN traffic. These commands have
been appended to the sshswitch startup script and applied live, restoring full
gateway reachability for both Campus-2 segments.
=== END ===
